    {
      "question": "e-474",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "12ab6e70ff36ed5fc6672e2352653efde5fb2c2103d9e5bfffba9d60bada41af",
      "new_sha256": "d9c35cfe0adb28a0e784861db36c1d45a9edfb3b0c49542c69a2e5ab475225b2",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-474",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006925/example-474.json",
          "file_sha256": "2cf8fdba454e5efb27b58c622b730f477ce82ca1ad68f95457734834988305c4"
        }
